  "final_answer": "If the backend endpoint `POST /api/asset` is deleted, it will be a **highly BREAKING change** for the Angular frontend. \n\nThe TypeScript consumers that would be broken and their impact are detailed below:\n\n---\n\n### 1. Direct TypeScript Consumer\n* **File**: `ui-ngx/src/app/core/http/asset.service.ts`\n* **Service Method**: `AssetService.saveAsset(asset, saveParams?, config?)`\n* **Code Code snippet**:\n  ```typescript\n  public saveAsset(asset: Asset, saveParams?: string | string[] | SaveEntityWithGroupParams, config?: RequestConfig): Observable<Asset> {\n    const params = toSaveParams(saveParams);\n    return this.http.post<Asset>('/api/asset', asset, createDefaultHttpOptions(params, config));\n  }\n  ```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Indirect TypeScript Consumers (Broken Downstream)\n\nBecause `AssetService.saveAsset` is part of the core entity management APIs, multiple services and table configs depend on it:\n\n#### A. Generic Entity Service\n* **File**: `ui-ngx/src/app/core/http/entity.service.ts`\n  * **`EntityService.saveEntity`**: Fails to save generic entities when the `EntityType` is `EntityType.ASSET`.\n    ```typescript\n    case EntityType.ASSET:\n      observable = this.assetService.saveAsset(entity as Asset, null, config);\n      break;\n    ```\n  ",
